    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-221775",
      "sequence_position": 6,
      "problem": "Disprove the following statement:\n\n$11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0$ for any $n\\in N.$",
      "worked_solution": "Our statement can be written as\n\n$$\n∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0.\n$$\n\nWe want to disprove this statement. In other words, we must show that its negation is true:\n\n$$\n¬(∀n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28\\le 0)\\,≡\\,∃n\\in N,\\,11n-{n}^{2}-28>0\n$$\n\nSo, we begin as follows:\n\nTo disprove a universal statement, it's sufficient to find a counterexample. In other words, we need to show that there exists a natural number $n$ such that \n\n$$\n11n-{n}^{2}-28>0.\n$$\n\nFinding a counterexample is sometimes a nontrivial task, but we only need one. In this case, substituting $n=5$ suffices:\n\nBy substituting $n=5$ into the expression, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11n-{n}^{2}-28 & =11(5)-(5)^{2}-28 \\\\  & =2 \\\\  & >0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we state our conclusion:\n\nSo, $n=5$ is our counterexample. Therefore, the given statement is false.",
      "answer_fields_complete": false,
      "result": "Correct",
      "grade": "Correct",
